247: cite36†Back cite35†Contact L248: \nL249: \nL250: \nL251: View Result for:\nL252: \nL253: [Select]\nL254: \nL255: /dtmb/services/print/glossary/glossary-of-printing-terms\nL256: Back to Department of Technology, Management & Budget\nL257: \nL258: cite37†Image: outline of the shape of Michigan state in teal color L259: \nL260: Search is currently unavailable. Please try again later.\nL261: ### Popular on michigan.gov\nL262: \nL263:   * cite38†Agriculture and Rural Development L264:   * cite39†Civil Rights L265:   * cite40†Environment L266:   * cite41†Health and Human Services L267:   * cite42†Natural Resources L268:   * cite43†Secretary of State L269: ### How Do I...\nL270: \nL271:   * cite44†Register to Vote†mvic.sos.state.mi.us L272:   * cite45†Renew My License Plate†dsvsesvc.sos.state.mi.us L273:   * cite46†View Assistance Programs L274: \nL275: Unsupported Browser Detected\nL276: \nL277: The web Browser you are currently using is unsupported, and some features of this site may not work as intended. Please update to a modern browser such as Chrome, Firefox or Edge to experience all features Michigan.gov has to offer.\nL278: \nL279: Supported Browsers\nL280: \nL281:   * cite47†Google Chrome†www.google.com L282:   * cite48†Safari†www.apple.com L283:   * cite49†Microsoft Edge†www.microsoft.com L284:   * cite50†Firefox†www.mozilla.org L285: \nL286: Glossary of Printing Terms\nL287: # Glossary of Printing Terms\nL288: \nL289: Bleed - An image or printed color that runs off the trimmed edge of a page. Bleeds are created by trimming the page after printing.\nL290: \nL291: \nL292: \nL293: CYMK - Cyan, magenta, yellow and black. The blending of these inks during printing creates full-color publications and images.\nL294: \nL295: \nL296: \nL297: Dummy - An unprinted mock-up of a book, brochure, or \"to-be-printed\" piece.\nL298: Duotone - A two-color halftone of an image created with two screens, two plates, and two colors. Duotones are made by printing an image with two colors, generally black and a second color. The full range of tones are printed black and the middle range of tones are printed in the second color. The result is a striking image with more richness and depth than a one-color halftone.\nL299: \nL300: \nL301: \nL302: Embossing - A shape is pressed into a sheet of paper with a metal or plastic die, creating a raised (embossed) image.\nL303: EPS - Encapsulated postscript file format. (See \"vector\")\nL304: \nL305: \nL306: \nL307: File Compression - Condensing a file so it takes up less space. (See \"lossless compression\" and \"lossy compression\").\nL308: Foil Stamping - An image created by covering paper with a thin, flexible sheet of metal or other material. The foil, which may be clear or opaque, comes in a range of colors and is carried on a plastic sheet. Stamping separates the foil from the plastic and makes it adhere to the paper. Foil stamping can be combined with embossing or debossing as an added design element.\nL309: Hickey - An irregularity in the ink coverage of a printed page. Hickeys are caused by paper or pressroom dust or dirt on the printing blanket which prevents the ink from adhering to the paper surface.\nL310: \nL311: \nL312: \nL313: Imposition - The arrangement of pages in a printing form so that, when the sheet they are printed on is folded and trimmed, the pages appear in proper sequence.\nL314: JPEG (.jpg) - Joint Photographic Expert Group. A lossy compression method used for storing large bitmap images and displaying images on the web.\nL315: \nL316: \nL317: \nL318: Kerning - Adjusting the space between characters.\nL319: \nL320: \nL321: \nL322: Leading - Space between lines of type. General rule of thumb is to add two points leading to your type size. For example, 11 point type with 13 point leading.\nL323: Lines Per Inch - The number of lines in an inch, as found on the screens that create halftones and four-color process images (for example, \"printed 175-line screen\"). The more lines per inch, the more detailed the printed image will be.\nL324: \nL325: \nL326: \nL327: Lossless Compression - A compression method that reduces the size of a file without any loss of data.\nL328: \nL329: \nL330: \nL331: Lossy Compression - A compression method that reduces the size of a file with varying or adjustable loss of data. JPEG is an example.\nL332: Moiré - A pattern created by printing several repetitive designs on top of each other. In multi-color printing, screens of colored dots print on top of each other. If the angles of the halftone screens of each of the colors are not properly aligned with each other, an undesirable, blurry pattern called \"moiré\" appears in the final image.\nL333: Resolution - A unit of measure. Image resolution measures the amount of data per inch in an image. Printer resolution measures the amount of detail that a printer or imagesetter can create. Scanner resolution measures the amount of detail that a scanner can capture.\nL334: \nL335: \nL336: \nL337: RGB - Red, Green, Blue. These are the colors that create image you see on your monitor.\nL338: \nL339: \nL340: \nL341: Pixel - PICture Element. The smallest unit of image data.\nL342: \nL343: \nL344: \nL345: Scoring - Pressing a \"channel\" into a sheet of paper to allow it to fold more easily.\nL346: \nL347: \nL348: Self-cover - A booklet having a cover made of the same paper as the inside or text pages.\nL349: \nL350: \nL351: \nL352: Vector - Drawn images, not photographic, composed of a series of points connected with straight or curved lines. These can be filled with color, gradients or patterns. These images are not subject to the resolution or sizing issues of bitmap images. They can be reduced or enlarged without losing image quality.\nL353: WYSIWYG - What You See Is What You Get. Software programs such as Adobe InDesign create publications that are WYSIWYG. Software programs such as Microsoft Publisher, Microsoft Word and Wordperfect are not WYSIWYG. Pages change dependent on font usage, print drivers, etc.\nL354: \nL355: \nL356: \nL357: cite51†Image: Department of Technology, Management and Budget L358: \nL359: Glossary of Printing Terms\nL360: \nL361: Copyright State of Michigan\nL362: \nL363: cite51†Image: Department of Technology, Management and Budget L364: \nL365: Sign-up for Email Alerts\nL366: \nL367: [Input]\nL368: \nL369: [Input]\nL370: [Input: Enter a valid email]\nL371: \nL372: [Button: Submit]\nL373: \nL374: Get personalized voter information on early voting and other topics.\nL375: \nL376: \nL377: \nL378: cite52†Michigan.gov/Vote L379: ## About\nL380: \nL381: Through our customer service, resource optimization and the innovative use of information and technology, DTMB touches every area of state government.  Our broad spectrum of responsibility, ranging from IT solutions to procurement and facilities management, improves how Michigan interacts with citizens and businesses every day.\nL382: \nL383: cite3†About DTMB L384: ## Job Openings\nL385: \nL386: Are you looking for a new career opportunity? DTMB provides our employees with challenging yet rewarding careers. We offer job stability, great benefits, flexible work schedules and opportunities for career advancement. If you are looking to make a difference and be part of something greater, apply TODAY! Search for DTMB careers below.\nL387: \nL388: cite4†DTMB Careers L389: \nL390: Popular Resources\nL391: \nL392:   * cite10†Legislative Reports L393:   * cite4†Career Opportunities L394: \nL395: Related Agencies\nL396:   * cite53†Michigan Center for Data and Analytics L397:   * cite54†Michigan's Public Safety Communications System L398:   * cite55†Office of the Child Advocate L399:   * cite56†Office of Retirement Services L400:   * cite57†Office of the State Employer L401:   * cite58†State Budget Office L402: \nL403: Policies\nL404: \nL405:   * cite59†Freedom of Information Act (FOIA) L406: \nL407: Copyright 2026 State of Michigan cite60†Mi.gov Home cite61†Policies cite62†Accessibility cite63†Disability Resources cite64†Language Services cite65†FOIA Directory cite66†Departments L408: \nL409: cite67†Image: Tracking Image --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOpening Artists’ Books | imposition (https://oab.lib.utah.edu/artist_book_term/imposition/)\nciteturn4view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view3\",\"lineno\":0}); Total lines: 37\nL0:   * cite0†About L1:   * cite1†Artists’ Book Terms L2:   * cite2†Suggest a Term L3:   * cite3†Feedback L4: \nL5:   * cite0†About L6:   * cite1†Artists’ Book Terms L7:   * cite2†Suggest a Term L8:   * cite3†Feedback L9: \nL10: # imposition\nL11: ## OAB Definition\nL12: \nL13: the process of planning the order of a book’s pages for most efficient and accurate printing and binding. Multiple Images and or units of text are printed on a printer’s sheet in a single run, often with a second run to print the backside of the sheet. The printer’s sheet is then cut into spreads, if needed, and folded into signatures or as appropriate to book structure.\nL14: ### Linked Terms\nL15: \nL16: RT cite4†folio L17: \nL18: RT cite5†French fold L19: \nL20: RT cite6†octavo L21: \nL22: RT cite7†quarto L23: \nL24: aat cite8†imposition†www.getty.edu L25: \nL26: lcsh cite9†Imposition (Printing)†id.loc.gov L27: \nL28: rbmscv cite10†Imposition†id.loc.gov L29: #### Additional Resources\nL30: \nL31:   * cite11†Wikipedia: Imposition†en.wikipedia.org L32:   * cite12†ThoughtCo.: Imposition for Commercial Print Jobs†www.thoughtco.com L33:   * cite13†Wikipedia: Letterpress printing- Imposition†en.wikipedia.org L34:   * cite14†Encyclopedia of Diderot & d’Alembert Collaborative Translation Project: Letterpress printing†quod.lib.umich.edu L35: \nL36: cite15†Image†